        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "An application message is divided into packets, and each packet is often divided again into flow-control digits called flits. The header flit identifies the destination. At each router, routing logic chooses an output direction, virtual-channel allocation reserves buffering, switch allocation grants passage, and the crossbar moves the flit onto the next link. These stages can overlap like an assembly line. The animation matters because latency is not one number: it is the sum of serialization, waiting, routing, switching, link travel, and possible contention at every hop."
        },
        "model": "edge-tts-7.2.8",
