        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "A modern many-core processor is not one giant calculator. It is a neighborhood of compute cores, cache banks, accelerators, and memory controllers that must exchange data. A request from one core may need a cache line stored several millimeters away. Shared buses become a bottleneck as core count rises, so designers place a small router beside each tile and connect those routers as a network-on-chip. The central question changes from, 'How fast is this core? ' to, 'How predictably can information move through the whole chip? '"
        },
