        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Now imagine two cores sending traffic through the same output port. Only one can win that cycle. Arbitration selects a packet; the other waits in a buffer. If downstream buffers fill, backpressure propagates toward the senders. This prevents data loss, but it can create long queues and head-of-line blocking. A useful analysis therefore asks four questions: where traffic is injected, which routes it follows, where paths overlap, and how flow control responds. Average latency alone can hide an unstable network whose tail latency grows sharply under bursts."
        },
