        "input": {
          "instructions": "Clear, measured, and instructor-ready.",
          "text": "Wireless links offer a different geometry. Instead of crossing many short router-to-router hops, a packet can use an on-chip antenna to take an express path across the die. That can reduce hop count and long-distance latency, especially for multicast or irregular communication. But the wireless medium is shared. Designers must coordinate access, control interference, budget transmitter energy, and decide which traffic deserves the shortcut. The right comparison is not 'wireless is faster. ' It is whether a hybrid network improves system-level latency and energy for the target workload without creating a new shared bottleneck."
        },
